5. P&A, Exception arg Hygiene:
    Phase 1:
    [x] 'ports.py'
        - 'getFileContent' was pruned, not used
    Phase 2:
    [x] 'app/exceptions.py': Prohibit arguments across all BaseEx exception classes
        - Override BaseEx.__init__ to convert any incoming args/kwargs into an IllegalExArgs exception with a synthetic ValueError cause
        - Enforce parameterless exception instantiation at runtime across the entire system
    [x] Move the prohibition of arguments up to BaseEx and integrate ExceptionPolicy
        - Consolidated ExceptionPolicy in app/exceptions.py alongside the exception hierarchy
        - Standardized ExceptionPolicy to treat all system exceptions as parameterless
        - utilizing __cause__ chaining for stack trace formatting and error diagnostics

    [x] 'ports_adapters/': align exception raising to exception policy. 
        - Align all raising of ex to policy, parameterless raising - always omitting parentheses
            - yes: raise ExName
            - also yes: raise ExName from InstantiatedValuePassingEg("Here we can put a little msg if we must")
            - no: raise ExName()
            - cause of exit 1 due to policy: raise ExName("we raise the ex!")
    Phase 3: All adapters must raise proper ex, located adjacent to their ports
        Note: LinuxDiskAdapter is deliberately deferred to later phase
        Static explainer: The ex they raise should be 'core near' in their semantics, so the adapters dont carry business rules
            - 'NoSuchFile' OK
            - 'IllegalDuplicateFile' - not OK
        [x] 'yaml_parser.py': came pre-aligned
        [x] 'scripted_terminal_adapter.py': Align test terminal adapter to exception policy
            - 'read_char': Handle script bounds; catch unexpected sequence access or EOF -> raise TerminalFailure from ex
            - '_flush_report': Wrap report file writing and directory creation (OSError, UnicodeError) -> raise TerminalFailure from ex
        [x] 'linux.py': Align terminal adapter exception handling to policy
            - Define 'TerminalFailure(Fatal)' in 'ports.py'
            - 'to_clipboard': Catch stream/encoding exceptions -> raise TerminalFailure from ex
            - 'write': Check non-zero exit code of 'os.system("clear")' -> raise TerminalFailure from ValueError(...); catch stdout write/flush exceptions -> raise TerminalFailure from ex
            - 'read_char': Catch termios and stdin stream errors (termios.error, OSError, EOFError) -> raise TerminalFailure from ex; check for unexpected empty EOF read -> raise TerminalFailure from ValueError(...)
            - 'get_acceptance': Propagate read_char exceptions naturally; catch stdout echo write/flush errors -> raise TerminalFailure from ex
        [x] 'ports.py'
            - declare new ex here - adjacent to their port following current structure of placement

    Phase 4: Core alignment to port adjacent ex 
        Note: DiskPort is deferred to phase 5 and not a concern
        Static explainer: The services should..
        - if ex is notice, then deal with it
        - if fatal, reraise / no need to listen
